  "reasoning": [
    "Accepted STP L>=5 has N=4L-2<=capacity. Write starts2, final four-byte write starts2+4*(L-2), its highest index N-1, and postincrementN is representable by clog2(capacity+1). EMIT stops on read_pos=N-1 before another increment. Header-derived expected length stays13 bits, so overlong headers cannot alias the bounded packet count.",
    "After j consumes each lane is its original128-bit value shifted right8*j. Constant low-byte selectors therefore equal the old word_index selectors. Payload acquisition needs !block_pending while consumption needs block_pending; shifts do not occur in LOOK acceptance or held EMIT. The successor is processed after output drains.",
    "Registered first byte is loaded at LOOK acceptance or DLLP end after packet[0] was stored in earlier cycles. Nonlast accepted output loads exactly packet[read_pos+1]. No ready handshake means no data or pointer update. Last output never indexes beyond packet length. Flush/start/abort masks stale data and the next packet reloads it.",
    "clog2(capacity) at power-of-two capacities is not necessarily a fault because accepted TLP counts are2mod4. Whole512-bit shift-right8 is observationally equivalent for the16 words; spill reaches each byte only after its last use. Duplicate consume task calls within one NBA cycle also do not prove double advancement. Those were not mislabeled rejected faults."
  ],
